1
00:00:01,950 --> 00:00:06,147
This paper puts a thought process
that stays small up against

2
00:00:06,147 --> 00:00:10,137
a task whose output has to stay
large — every pixel of it.

3
00:00:11,817 --> 00:00:17,279
The encoder does the heavy lifting
first, in the style SwinIR established.

4
00:00:18,783 --> 00:00:23,708
Then the field is cut into windows, and
nothing is pooled away on the way through.

5
00:00:25,217 --> 00:00:29,454
The thought machine sits here in the
middle, working on what the windows hold.

6
00:00:30,950 --> 00:00:35,600
Only at the very end does the
decoder upsample, four times over.

7
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:39,612
That loop is a single tick, and it runs in

8
00:00:39,612 --> 00:00:42,400
place rather than around
the whole pipeline.

9
00:00:43,283 --> 00:00:45,998
Every tick reuses the same weights, so

10
00:00:45,998 --> 00:00:49,283
thinking for longer costs
time and not memory.

11
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:54,313
Look at the bottom row of the
comparison figure — all three

12
00:00:54,313 --> 00:00:57,675
approaches differ only in what
they hand the thought block.

13
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:01,787
The windows come first: the field is

14
00:01:01,787 --> 00:01:05,437
partitioned, and each one
is handled on its own.

15
00:01:06,483 --> 00:01:10,908
The block in the middle is shared,
the same weights for every window.

16
00:01:11,983 --> 00:01:16,820
And what comes out is still a dense field,
one query for every position in it.

17
00:01:18,517 --> 00:01:21,279
Picture the feature map
as a grid of tokens.

18
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:25,337
Attention runs inside
one window at a time.

19
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:31,612
The next window does the same, and
at this stage the two never mix.

20
00:01:32,717 --> 00:01:36,554
Count the tokens before a tick and
after it and you get the same number.

21
00:01:39,050 --> 00:01:43,375
The encoder turns the low-resolution
image into a dense feature field.

22
00:01:45,383 --> 00:01:50,033
The partition then cuts that field into
windows without throwing any of it away.

23
00:01:52,083 --> 00:01:57,008
The window-wise version collapses each
window down to a single summary token.

24
00:01:58,017 --> 00:02:03,567
This one asks a question at every position
instead, and the token count survives it.

25
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:06,483
The partition is identical in both.

26
00:02:06,483 --> 00:02:10,158
What differs is how much of it
the thought state ever sees.

27
00:02:12,617 --> 00:02:16,629
These are five-benchmark averages
at four times upscaling.

28
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:21,600
CATANet is the one to beat, at 29.48.

29
00:02:22,917 --> 00:02:26,460
The method here comes
in at 28.98 — ahead of

30
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:29,842
two CNN baselines, behind the transformer.

31
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:33,937
Only that last row was
trained for this paper.

32
00:02:33,937 --> 00:02:35,637
Every other number is quoted.

33
00:02:38,717 --> 00:02:41,842
The first tick is worth
almost a full decibel.

34
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:45,724
The fourth is worth under
a tenth, and training

35
00:02:45,724 --> 00:02:48,887
stopped there, so nothing
past it is demonstrated.
